cinematix samurai are known for their  fierce loyalty deadly precision with the  sword and an unswerving commitment to a  code of honor from Japanese classics to  American takes these are the samurai  movies you absolutely need to watch  before you die the name that rightly  dominates any discussion of great  samurai movies is Akira Kurosawa the  late Japanese director made some of the  best samurai movies ever and he's  generally regarded as one of the most  influential filmmakers of all time I  think he comes from a generation of  filmmakers that were still influenced by  silent films the film most often  associated with Kurosawa is 1954 Seven  Samurai the heroes of this epic film are  Ronin which are samurai without masters  the seven warriors unite to protect a  farming community from merciless bandits  they also train the villagers into a  makeshift militia and the whole thing  culminates in a huge bloody battle if  the plot of Seven Samurai sounds  familiar that's because it's been copied  over and over again a classic Western  the Magnificent Seven is a remake of  Seven Samurai and plenty of other  westerns have found similar inspiration  the story of veteran heroes training  peaceful towns folk into soldiers has  been copied in everything from comedies  like three amigos to episodes of shows  like the Clone Wars and the mandalorian  and the trope of assembling a team has  echoes in heist films like Ocean's  eleven war movies like The Dirty Dozen  and superhero flicks like the Avengers  basically if you love movies then you've  almost definitely seen something  influenced by Kurosawa if you're a fan  of westerns you're likely familiar with  1992's  Unforgiven Clint Eastwood's  oscar-winning film about an aging  gunslinger who sets out on one last job  I'll see you in Hell with money  but what you may not know is that Korean  director Lee Sang el remade Unforgiven  in 2013 with Japanese actor Ken Watanabe  as retired samurai Jubei said in Japan's  Hokkaido frontier the plot of 2013's  unforgiven Hughes pretty closely to the  originals  Jubei and his old friend King go are  lured out of retirement by the promise  of a handsome reward for killing two  brothers who have disfigured a sex  worker they're joined by the younger  Goro who makes bold claims about a  triumphant and violent past  the trio clash with the local lawman  who's determined to stop anyone looking  to make good on the sex workers contract  this is clearly destined to end badly  for everyone involved  Unforgiven is a visually rich morally  challenging epic it's a faithful echo of  its source material with the added  benefit of Watanabe a singular talent  2002's the twilight samurai is a  different kind of samurai movie its hero  say BAE  Iguchi is a low-level poor samurai who  works as a clerk his wife dies early in  the film leaving him to raise his two  daughters alone on a modest stipend when  his old friend Tomo begins to regularly  visit him and help take care of the  girls say BAE falls in love sadly though  he is ashamed of his social status and  initially refuses to marry his old  friend while the Twilight samurai has  its share of swordplay neither action  nor adventures the focus of the film  it's more interested in character than  other typical elements of the genre and  as such say BAE is a lot more relatable  than your average fictional samurai most  samurai movie fans probably don't deal  with a lot of far-reaching adventures  involving assassination and revenge in  their own lives but things like loss  love and the difficult balance between  work and family are much more Universal  concepts the Twilight samurai is a  refreshing and uncharacteristically  upbeat samurai film and that distinction  helped it earn a dozen Japanese Academy  Awards along with an Oscar nomination  for Best Foreign Language Film in the  wake of the Twilight samurai success in  2002 the doors opened for more  filmmakers to dive back into the  long-neglected genre among them was  Shameka who earned praise for his 2010  remake of the 1963 classic 13 assassins  said in 1844 the story follows a group  of samurai determined to take down a  powerful and cruel Lord whose rule could  mean nothing but horror for the future  of Japan despite its direct source  material 13 assassins often feels a lot  like Seven Samurai including its length  at close to two and a half hours 13  assassins begins relatively slowly but  climaxes with a spectacular and  prolonged battle between the heroes and  the villains army it's an epic set piece  that includes an abandoned town  converted into a deadly labyrinth of  booby traps the film is heralded as one  of me Kay's best and it also went a long  way to revitalize the doormat samurai  genre it was nominated for 10 Japanese  Academy Awards and won four of them and  a spectrum culture critic David Harris  wrote me Kay has created a reverential  and vital film in a genre that many may  have thought as moribund and passe as  its subject the plays of William  Shakespeare have remained relevant for  centuries after they were first written  his works have been adapted into  countless movies because they still  speak to audiences today Akira Kurosawa  proved this better than anyone with 1957  s throne of blood his adaptation of  Shakespeare's tragedy Macbeth while  Macbeth is set in Scotland throne of  blood takes place in Japan but besides  the setting change the two stories are  otherwise remarkably similar throne of  bloods protagonist is general washi zu  who at first seems as loyal to his Lord  as any samurai after he's visited by a  strange spirit who tells him that one  day he'll become the lord of spider's  web Castle he tells his wife asaji about  the prophecy just as Lady Macbeth did  with her husband asaji urges her partner  to make the spirits prediction come true  by killing the Lord and taking his place  after that  just like his Shakespearean inspiration  washi zu finds himself well past the  point of no return and on a road that  can only end with a bloodbath  throne of blood is not only essential  for samurai  but for Shakespeare devotees as well as  the Guardian critic Derek Malcolm put it  it remains a landmark of visual strength  and is possibly the finest Shakespearean  adaptation ever committed to the screen  this clever re-imagination of a  Shakespearean classic is always included  among Kurosawa's best one of the most  unique entries on this list is Jim  Jarmusch's 1999 crime drama Ghost Dog  the way of the samurai this one doesn't  take place in Japan and stranger still  we're not sure exactly where in America  it's meant to be set as no city name is  ever provided within the film itself  Forest Whitaker stars as the titular hit  man who binds himself in service to  local mobster Louis after Louis saves  his life but Ghost Dog isn't just any  old hitman when he's not working he  tends to his pigeons meditates and  studies hagakure a collection of  conversations from the early 18th  century about the meaning of the code of  bushido ghost dog sees himself as a  samurai and is a strict follower of  Bushido follow a code I've always given  you I respect the fact that Ghost Dog  continues to work for Louis and  eventually begins taking out his bosses  competition when Louie's life is in  danger has proof enough of his devotion  to the code even after it becomes clear  that Louie isn't returning Ghost Dog  loyalty the hit man refuses to lift a  hand against him ultimately Ghost Dog  the way of the samurai is a uniquely  moving and surprisingly funny film about  a man who refuses to let go of who he is  in the face of a hostile world by the  early 1960s Japanese actor Toshiro  Mifune had already starred in multiple  Akira Kurosawa films in 1961 the two of  them teamed up once again for the first  of a pair of films in which me foon a  played impoverished samurais Sandra Dahl  a name that means 30 years old each  movie would prove to be some of the best  in the entire Canon of the genre when we  first meet Sun Judo in Yojimbo he's  found himself in a town where a feud  between two warring clans is raging  rather than work for one clan or the  other he winds up getting hired by both  sides as part of a secret plan that he  hopes will wipe out both groups  in 1962 Mifune starred in the follow-up  sanju doe in which the title hero teams  up with a group of samurai intent on  fighting a corrupt board some of the  most influential aspects of Yojimbo and  sandra are felt in western cinema  Yojimbo antihero is credited as the  prototype for the similarly immoral man  with no name as played by Clint Eastwood  in Italian director Sergio Leone a  spaghetti-western dollars trilogy the  similarities between these two  characters aren't especially clear in  the more comedic saundra but in a much  darker Yojimbo their shared  characteristics are obvious enough for  anyone to recognize Kill Bill is a  stylistically beautiful revenge epic and  a story too big for a single film it's  also Quentin Tarantino's love letter to  martial arts and samurais cinema in  2003's Kill Bill Vol 1  uma thurman's merciless ex assassin is  known only as the bride when we first  meet her we have no idea why she's  arrived at Vernita green suburban home  in a garish yellow truck or why the two  women perceived to beat the crap out of  each other you can take my word for it  your mother had it coming eventually we  learned that the bride left the employ  of the enigmatic bill to pursue a normal  life but at the rehearsal for her  wedding bill found her and he and his  top assassins killed all of the guests  and left the bride for dead miraculously  though she survived and began her path  of vengeance the first film is heavier  on action particularly in the climax in  the Japanese restaurant the house of  blue leaves it's there that the bride  has a darkly hilarious and visually  stunning showdown against the sword  wielding crazy 88 Kill Bill Vol 2  arrived in two theaters just one year  later it dives deeper into the bride's  background and the reasons for her war  with bill the entire to film saga is a  wonderful cinematic achievement that all  action movie fanatics should experience  at least once American audiences may not  be familiar with the name zato Ichi  but in Japan the fictional blind  swordsman is eminently recognizable  between 1962 and 1989 Japanese actor  Shintaro katsu  starred as zato Ichi in 26 films along  with 100 episodes of the zato Ichi TV  series that aired from 1974 to 1979 in  2003 Takeshi Kitano revitalized the hero  with the blind swordsman zato Ichi which  he directed wrote and starred in as the  legendary samurai character in the film  zato Ichi is an otherwise peaceful  swordsman who helps a pair of sisters in  their quest to make criminals pay for  killing their parents the movie is a  series of contradictions that shouldn't  work together but somehow they do it's  as bloody as a typical samurai film  while it also manages to be hilarious  and often beautiful gitanos unique style  makes these warring notes work perfectly  with big laughs and bigger stylized  bloodletting as time out critic Jeff  Andrews put it the blind swordsman zato  Ichi is without doubt fast funny  fabulous to behold in many ways 1965  samurai assassin is a tragedy of  Shakespearean proportions its hero is  pseudo geol needle Aronian who knows  he's the illegitimate son of a powerful  nobleman desperate to be recognized as a  true samurai he joins a plot to  assassinate a controversial Lord he  hopes that this will earn his father's  pride and encourage him to reveal  himself based on an actual incident in  Japanese history samurai assassin can at  times get bogged down with the  exposition that provides historical  context but its payoff is a stunning  brutal battle in the snow at the end of  the film which makes the wait more than  worth it fans of happy endings may want  to skip samurai assassin as it includes  a surprise twist that makes everything  that unfolds seem even starker and more  senseless regardless it's a captivating  presentation of tragic historical events  that eventually lead to the end of the  samurai check out one of our newest  videos right here plus even more looper  videos about your favorite stuff are  coming soon subscribe to our YouTube  channel and hit the belt so you don't  miss a single one  you  [Music]
 

